00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:23.680
Imagine you already own a TFSA

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:26.440
and want to add real estate exposure

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:28.300
without buying a second property.

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:32.160
You could purchase units of a publicly traded REIT

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:36.600
such as Reocann or Canadian apartment REIT at Market Open.

00:08:37.300 --> 00:08:39.240
The order fills instantly

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:42.700
but the distribution yield in the 4-6% range

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:44.800
for many names arrives monthly

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:49.660
and is taxed as ordinary income inside an RRSP.

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:53.940
The mechanism that surprises most retail investors

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:55.980
is the tax treatment difference.

00:08:56.820 --> 00:09:00.740
REIT distributions are not eligible for the dividend tax credit.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:06.720
Inside a TFSA or RRSP, they grow tax-deferred.

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:10.640
Direct rental properties allow depreciation claims

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:14.120
and potential principal residence exemption on sale.

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:20.200
A pro tip is to check the REIT's AFFO payout ratio before buying.

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:25.300
Anything above 90% leaves little cushion for maintenance capex.

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:30.460
Mortgage investment corporations offer another route with higher yields

00:09:30.460 --> 00:09:35.580
but illiquidity and credit risk that retail platforms rarely highlight.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:39.380
The biggest mistake with real estate allocation

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:43.120
is treating your home and the REIT as interchangeable.

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:47.020
Instead, always size the REIT or MIC position

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:49.300
so that total real estate exposure,

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:51.980
including your principal residence equity,

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:55.080
stays below 30% to 40% of net worth.

